===Etymology 2===
From the mascot of Princeton (a tiger), which led to early cheerleaders calling out "Tiger" at the end of a cheer for the Princeton team.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# A final shouted phrase, accompanied by a jump or outstretched arms, at the end of a cheer.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1868 |title=Punch: Or the London Charivari - Volume 55 |author=|page=231 |ISBN=|passage=He spoke with a very strong Scotch accent, and is by no means a graceful orator, but he produced througout a most favourable impression upon all his hearers, and especially upon the students, one of whom shouted as the speaker closed, 'Long Live PRESIDENT M'COSH!' and then proposed three cheers, which were given with a will, followed by the usual '''tiger''' and ' rocket.' }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1941|title=Reveille in Washington|author=Margaret Leech|page= |ISBN=|passage=. . . every blue coat in the audience sprang to his feet, with three times three and a '''tiger'''. }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2008 |title=The Outdoor Handy Book: For Playground, Field, and Forest |author=D. C. Beard |page=413 |ISBN=|passage=One Brooklyn military company has a “'''tiger'''” composed of a provincial expression borrowed from the farmers. When drawled out by a hundred throats the phrase "I-wanter-know!" always produces a laugh.}}

